window.READING_EMBEDDED_MD = {
  "5l-mvr": "# ⚡ Five-Level Multiple-Pole VIENNA Rectifier (5L-MVR): Applied Design & Modulation Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: G. Ooi H. P., A. I. Maswood, Z. Lim, and M. A. Chaitanya, *\"Input Current Shaping of Five-Level Multiple-Pole VIENNA Rectifier Topologies with Reduced Component and Better Performance,\"* IEEE, pp. 900–905, 2013.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Engineering Problem & When to Use 5L-MVR\n\nIn medium-to-high-power AC-DC front-end drives (e.g., industrial motor drives, telecom rectifiers, EV fast-charger front-ends), conventional 3-level VIENNA or NPC rectifiers require high PWM switching frequencies ($10\\text{--}20\\text{ kHz}$) to keep input line inductor ($L_{a,b,c}$) volume and input current $\\text{THD}_i$ low. That high switching frequency creates severe $dv/dt$ stress, EMI filtering overhead, and switching losses.\n\nThe **5L-MVR** solves this by synthesizing a **5-level stepped input voltage** ($+V_{dc}/2, +V_{dc}/4, 0, -V_{dc}/4, -V_{dc}/2$) using **only 6 active switches total** (2 per phase: one **Inner Cell** switch $S_{x1}$ and one **Outer Cell** switch $S_{x2}$), operating at **1 kHz** outer-cell PWM combined with fundamental-frequency inner-cell angle firing ($\\alpha = 30^\\circ$).\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Mains[\"3-Phase AC Mains\"]\n        Vabc[\"V_a, V_b, V_c\"] --> Lf[\"Small Input Inductors L_a, L_b, L_c\"]\n    end\n    subgraph Cells[\"Per-Phase Dual-Cell Stage (x = a, b, c)\"]\n        Lf --> Outer[\"Outer Cell (Switch Sx2, Diodes Dx7-Dx12)\\n1 kHz LS-PWM or PS-PWM\\nMax Blocking = V_dc / 2\"]\n        Outer --> Inner[\"Inner Cell (Switch Sx1, Diodes Dx1-Dx6)\\nFundamental Angle Firing (α = 30°)\\nMax Blocking = V_dc / 4\"]\n    end\n    subgraph DCBus[\"4-Capacitor Split DC Bus\"]\n        Inner --> Caps[\"C1, C2, C3, C4 (Each V_dc / 4)\"]\n        Caps --> Bal[\"Active DC/DC Voltage Balancer (Q1-Q4, L1, L2)\"]\n        Bal --> Load[\"Regulated DC Bus V_dc\"]\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Governing Equations for Hardware Sizing\n\n### 2.1 Five-Level Pole Voltage & Modulation Index\nWith a 4-capacitor split DC link ($E = V_{dc}/4$ per capacitor):\n$$v_{xN} \\in \\left\\{ +2E,\\; +E,\\; 0,\\; -E,\\; -2E \\right\\} = \\left\\{ +\\frac{V_{dc}}{2},\\; +\\frac{V_{dc}}{4},\\; 0,\\; -\\frac{V_{dc}}{4},\\; -\\frac{V_{dc}}{2} \\right\\}$$\n\nThe AC-to-DC modulation index $M$ relates peak phase voltage $\\hat{V}_x$ to total DC bus voltage $V_{dc}$:\n$$M = \\frac{2\\,\\hat{V}_x}{V_{dc}}, \\qquad 0.5 < M \\le 1.0$$\n\n### 2.2 Optimal Firing Angle ($\\alpha$) Selection\n- **Inner Cell ($S_{x1}$)**: Conducts from $\\alpha$ to $\\pi - \\alpha$ in each half-cycle, triggered by a grid zero-crossing PLL.\n- **Outer Cell ($S_{x2}$)**: Modulated at $1\\text{ kHz}$ using either **Level-Shifted PWM (LS-PWM)** or **Phase-Shifted PWM (PS-PWM)**.\n- **Design Sweet Spot** *(verify in the Live Simulator above)*:\n  - **LS-PWM**: $\\alpha_{\\text{opt}} = 30^\\circ$ yields $\\text{PF} \\approx 0.999$ and minimum $\\text{THD}_i = 3.43\\%$.\n  - **PS-PWM**: $\\alpha_{\\text{opt}} = 25^\\circ$ yields $\\text{PF} \\approx 0.999$ and minimum $\\text{THD}_i = 3.60\\%$.\n\n### 2.3 Capacitor Voltage Balancing & Power-Balance Loop\nThe outer-loop PI controller regulates total $V_{dc}$ by scaling the sinusoidal current reference $i_{x,\\text{ref}} = I_{\\text{peak}}\\sin(\\omega t - \\phi_x)$ in phase with $v_x$, while the auxiliary buck-boost balancing stage ($Q_1\\text{--}Q_4, L_1, L_2$) equalizes $V_{C1}=V_{C2}=V_{C3}=V_{C4}=V_{dc}/4$.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Applied Engineering Design & Commissioning Checklist\n\n| Step | Engineering Action | Target / Verification Metric |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Switch Voltage Rating** | Select $S_{x1}$ (Inner) for $\\ge 1.5\\times (V_{dc}/4)$ and $S_{x2}$ (Outer) for $\\ge 1.5\\times (V_{dc}/2)$ | Inner switches need half the blocking rating of outer switches |\n| **2. Zero-Crossing PLL** | Implement clean hardware/digital ZCD with hysteresis for $\\alpha$ timer | Phase jitter $< 0.2^\\circ$ to prevent even-harmonic injection |\n| **3. Modulation Mode** | Choose **LS-PWM** with $\\alpha = 30^\\circ$ for lowest THD; use **PS-PWM** with $\\alpha = 25^\\circ$ if thermal sharing between outer devices is prioritized | Confirm $\\text{THD}_i < 5\\%$ (IEEE 519 compliance) at $f_{\\text{sw}} = 1\\text{ kHz}$ |\n| **4. DC-Link Balancer** | Verify $C_1\\text{--}C_4$ imbalance $< 2\\%$ under step load changes | Prevents asymmetric stepped levels ($+E$ vs $-E$) |\n| **5. Dead-Time & Snubbers** | Because VIENNA cells are unidirectional, shoot-through is structurally impossible; only diode reverse-recovery $di/dt$ snubbing is needed | Inspect $v_{CE}$ overshoot at turn-off |\n",
  "vr-dpmsm": "# ⚙️ Vienna Rectifier Front-End Dual Three-Phase PMSM Drive: Applied Control Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: S. Ohno, M. Haider, and S. Mirić, *\"Vienna Rectifier Front-End Dual Three-Phase PMSM Drive with Synergetic Control,\"* IEEE ICRERA, 2025.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Architecture & Engineering Motivation\n\nIndustrial high-power drives increasingly pair a **3-phase Vienna Rectifier (VR)** active front-end with an **asymmetrical dual three-phase PMSM** ($30^\\circ$ phase shift between winding sets $ABC$ and $XYZ$, isolated neutrals). This architecture reduces DC-link capacitance, splits phase current across six inverter legs, and provides fault tolerance.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Grid[\"3-Phase Grid (400 V_rms)\"] --> VR[\"Hybrid SiC/GaN Vienna Rectifier\\n3/3 PWM or 1/3 Clamping PWM\"]\n    VR -->|\"Split DC Bus (+/- 400 V)\"| Inv[\"6-Phase Two-Level Inverter\\n(Winding Set ABC + XYZ, 30° Shift)\"]\n    Inv --> Motor[\"Asymmetrical Dual 3-Phase PMSM\"]\n    Motor -->|\"VSD Transform\"| VSD[\"Vector Space Decomposition\\n(alpha-beta Torque + x-y Harmonic)\"]\n    VSD --> Syn[\"Synergetic Controller\\nMacro-variable Psi_T = 0\"]\n    Syn --> Inv\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Front-End Modulation Trade-Off: 3/3 PWM vs. 1/3 Six-Step Clamping\n\nIn the live simulator above, switch between **Mode 1 (3/3 PWM)** and **Mode 2/3 (1/3 PWM)** to observe how discontinuous clamping reduces switching loss:\n\n1. **3/3 Continuous PWM (Mode 1)**:\n   - All three phases switch continuously over the full $360^\\circ$ grid cycle.\n   - Lowest current ripple, but highest semiconductor switching loss.\n2. **1/3 Six-Step Clamping PWM (Modes 2 & 3)**:\n   - In each $60^\\circ$ sector, the phase carrying the **largest absolute current** is clamped directly to the $+V_{dc}/2$ or $-V_{dc}/2$ rail (zero switching transitions for $1/3$ of the fundamental period).\n   - **Result**: Switching losses drop by **$\\sim 33\\%$** while maintaining $\\text{PF} > 0.99$ and low $\\text{THD}_i$, at the expense of a slight increase in mid-point capacitor ripple.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dual 3-Phase PMSM Vector Space Decomposition (VSD) & Synergetic Control\n\n### 3.1 VSD Subspaces\nUsing the $6\\times 6$ Clarke-style VSD transformation, the 6 stator currents map into three orthogonal planes:\n- **$\\alpha\\text{-}\\beta$ (Fundamental Torque Plane)**: Maps into rotating $d\\text{-}q$ frame for flux ($i_d = 0$ MTPA) and electromagnetic torque ($T_e \\propto i_q$).\n- **$x\\text{-}y$ (Harmonic Loss Plane)**: Carries $5\\text{th}, 7\\text{th}, 17\\text{th}, 19\\text{th}$ harmonics that produce **zero torque** and pure copper loss—must be actively regulated to $i_x^* = i_y^* = 0$.\n- **$z_1\\text{-}z_2$ (Zero-Sequence)**: Blocked when neutrals $N_1, N_2$ are isolated.\n\n### 3.2 Nonlinear Synergetic Control Law vs. Linear PI\nStandard decoupled PI current loops suffer from phase lag and cross-coupling ($\\omega_e L_q i_q$, $\\omega_e L_d i_d$) at high electrical frequencies (e.g., $300\\text{ Hz}$). Synergetic control defines a macro-variable manifold $\\Psi_T = i_q^* - i_q$ and enforces exponential convergence:\n$$T\\,\\dot{\\Psi}_T + \\Psi_T = 0 \\implies v_q = R_s i_q + \\omega_e(L_d i_d + \\lambda_m) + L_q\\left(\\dot{i}_q^* + \\frac{i_q^* - i_q}{T}\\right)$$\nwhere **$T$** is the designer-chosen convergence time constant:\n- **Smaller $T$**: Faster step rise time and near-zero tracking phase error at $300\\text{ Hz}$ sinusoidal torque commands, but higher transient $v_q$ control effort.\n- **Larger $T$**: Smoother voltage demand, slower step settling.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Practical Commissioning Guide\n\n1. **Neutral Isolation**: Keep the two 3-phase star neutrals ($N_1, N_2$) isolated so $z_1\\text{-}z_2$ circulating currents are eliminated by hardware.\n2. **Dead-Time Compensation**: Because the $x\\text{-}y$ plane impedance is purely $R_s + s L_{ls}$ (very small inductance), inverter dead-time distortion easily excites large $i_x, i_y$ currents; always include dead-time compensation and active $x\\text{-}y$ current regulation.\n3. **Tuning $T$**: Start with $T \\approx 2\\text{--}3\\times T_s$ (sampling periods) and decrease $T$ until inverter DC-bus voltage saturation is approached during worst-case torque steps.\n",
  "wpcb-copper": "# ♻️ Hydrometallurgical Copper Recovery from Waste PCBs: Pre-Treatment & TOPSIS Optimization Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: M. Gómez, A. Barnadas-Rodríguez, C. Domínguez, L. Asensio, and J. L. Valiente, *\"From e-waste to resource: Identifying the optimal pre-treatment for copper recovery from waste PCBs,\"* *Resources, Conservation & Recycling*, vol. 228, 108821, 2026.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Engineering Context: Why Pre-Treatment Governs Leaching Economics\n\nWaste Printed Circuit Boards (WPCBs) contain $15\\text{--}30\\text{ wt\\%}$ copper alongside solder ($\\text{Sn/Pb}$), steel/aluminum hardware ($\\text{Fe/Al}$), and epoxy solder-mask polymers. Direct acid leaching of un-pretreated PCBs wastes reagents on base-metal impurities and suffers from mass-transfer blockage by the polymer solder mask.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    WPCB[\"Sorted Waste PCBs (Components Removed)\"] --> PT{\"Select Pre-Treatment Route\"}\n    PT -->|\"R0\"| Milled[\"Direct Mechanical Milling\\nHigh Yield, High Fe/Al/Sn Impurity\"]\n    PT -->|\"R1\"| NaOH[\"NaOH Solder Mask Stripping\\nLow Cost, Highest Cu Purity (~90%+)\"]\n    PT -->|\"R2\"| Thermal[\"Thermal Shock (Cryo/Heat)\\nModerate Delamination\"]\n    PT -->|\"R3\"| scCO2[\"Supercritical CO2 (scCO2)\\nCleanest Polymer Removal, High CapEx\"]\n    Milled & NaOH & Thermal & scCO2 --> Leach[\"Acid Leaching: HCl vs H2SO4 + H2O2\"]\n    Leach --> TOPSIS[\"TOPSIS Multi-Criteria Ranking (C_i*)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Analytical Framework & TOPSIS Equations\n\n### 2.1 Mass Loss & Leaching Recovery Metrics\n$$\\text{Mass Loss (\\%)} = \\frac{m_{\\text{initial}} - m_{\\text{final}}}{m_{\\text{initial}}} \\times 100, \\qquad \\eta_{\\text{Cu}}(t) = \\frac{C_{\\text{Cu}}(t)\\,V_{\\text{leach}}}{m_{\\text{Cu,total}}} \\times 100\\%$$\n\n### 2.2 TOPSIS Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA)\nTo balance competing plant KPIs—**Copper Purity ($C_1$)**, **Copper Yield ($C_2$)**, **Leaching Time ($C_3$)**, **Operating Cost ($C_4$)**, and **Environmental Impact ($C_5$)**—TOPSIS normalizes the decision matrix $r_{ij}$, applies weights $w_j$ ($\\sum w_j = 1$), and computes Euclidean distances to the Positive Ideal ($A^+$) and Negative Ideal ($A^-$) solutions:\n$$v_{ij} = w_j \\frac{x_{ij}}{\\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^m x_{ij}^2}}, \\qquad D_i^+ = \\sqrt{\\sum_j (v_{ij} - v_j^+)^2}, \\qquad D_i^- = \\sqrt{\\sum_j (v_{ij} - v_j^-)^2}$$\n$$C_i^* = \\frac{D_i^-}{D_i^+ + D_i^-}, \\qquad 0 \\le C_i^* \\le 1$$\nHigher **$C_i^*$** indicates the best overall engineering compromise.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Acid System & Process Selection Matrix\n\n| Process Parameter | $\\text{HCl}$ Leaching System | $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ System | Engineering Recommendation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base-Metal Co-Dissolution** | Dissolves $\\text{Sn, Pb, Fe, Al, Zn}$ rapidly | Selective toward $\\text{Cu}$ when $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ oxidant is controlled | Use $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4\\text{-H}_2\\text{O}_2$ for downstream electrowinning purity |\n| **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ Decomposition** | N/A | Catalyzed by $\\text{Fe}^{3+}$ and heat; requires staged dosing | Dose $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$ incrementally at $30\\text{--}45^\\circ\\text{C}$ |\n| **Best Pre-Treatment** | **NaOH Stripping** removes solder mask without $\\text{Fe/Al}$ pulverization | **NaOH Stripping** achieves $>90\\%$ leachate Cu purity | Use the **TOPSIS Weight Sliders** above to test your plant's cost vs. purity priorities |\n",
  "power-supplies": "# 🔋 Switching Power Supplies A to Z: Resonant (LLC), Dual Active Bridge (DAB) & Loop Compensation Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: S. Maniktala and N. Rosano, *\"Switching Power Supplies A to Z (3rd Edition Add-On),\"* Chapters 1–5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chapter 1: LLC Resonant Converter Design Workflow\n\nThe LLC resonant tank uses series inductance $L_r$, resonant capacitor $C_r$, and transformer magnetizing inductance $L_m$ ($\\kappa = L_m / L_r$) to achieve **Zero-Voltage Switching (ZVS)** on primary MOSFETs and **Zero-Current Switching (ZCS)** on secondary rectifiers.\n\n### 1.1 First-Harmonic Approximation (FHA) Voltage Gain\nDefine normalized frequency $f_n = f_{\\text{sw}} / f_r$ where $f_r = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L_r C_r}}$, inductance ratio $\\kappa = L_m / L_r$, and quality factor $Q = \\frac{\\sqrt{L_r / C_r}}{R_{ac}}$ with $R_{ac} = \\frac{8 n^2}{\\pi^2} R_{\\text{load}}$:\n$$M(f_n, \\kappa, Q) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\kappa}\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{f_n^2}\\right)\\right)^2 + Q^2\\left(f_n - \\frac{1}{f_n}\\right)^2}}$$\n\n### 1.2 Practical LLC Design Rules\n1. **Nominal Operation at $f_n = 1.0$ ($f_{\\text{sw}} = f_r$)**: Gain is load-independent ($M = 1.0$), minimizing circulating tank current and maximizing efficiency.\n2. **ZVS Inductive Region**: Always keep the minimum operating frequency above the peak gain boundary ($f_{\\text{min}} > f_{\\text{peak}}(Q_{\\text{max}})$) so tank current lags tank voltage ($\\text{ZVS}$). Entering the capacitive region causes shoot-through and body-diode reverse-recovery failure.\n3. **Choosing $\\kappa = L_m / L_r$**: Smaller $\\kappa$ ($3\\text{--}5$) gives higher boost gain for wide input/hold-up range, at the cost of higher magnetizing circulating current.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Chapter 2: Dual Active Bridge (DAB) Bidirectional Power Transfer\n\nThe DAB connects two full bridges across an isolated high-frequency transformer with total leakage inductance $L_s$ and phase-shift angle $\\phi$ (Single-Phase Shift, SPS):\n$$P_{\\text{DAB}}(\\phi) = \\frac{n V_{\\text{in}} V_{\\text{out}}}{2 \\pi^2 f_{\\text{sw}} L_s}\\,\\phi\\left(\\pi - |\\phi|\\right), \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\phi \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$$\n\n- **Peak Power**: Occurs at $|\\phi| = 90^\\circ$ ($\\pi/2$), though practical designs operate at $|\\phi| \\le 45^\\circ\\text{--}60^\\circ$ to limit RMS circulating current.\n- **ZVS Boundary**: Full-range ZVS on both bridges is naturally achieved when the voltage conversion ratio $d = \\frac{n V_{\\text{out}}}{V_{\\text{in}}} \\approx 1.0$. At light load or $d \\neq 1$, Triangular/Trapezoidal or Dual/Triple Phase Shift (DPS/TPS) restores ZVS.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Chapters 3–5: Small-Signal Modeling, Type-III Compensation & Digital PID Q-Matching\n\n### 3.1 Voltage-Mode Buck Plant ($L C$ Filter)\n$$G_{vd}(s) = V_{\\text{in}} \\frac{1 + s R_{\\text{ESR}} C}{1 + s\\left(\\frac{L}{R_{\\text{load}}} + R_{\\text{ESR}} C\\right) + s^2 L C}, \\qquad f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L C}}, \\qquad f_{\\text{ESR}} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi R_{\\text{ESR}} C}$$\n\n### 3.2 K-Factor Type-III Compensator Placement\nFor a target crossover frequency $f_c$ and phase boost $\\phi_{\\text{boost}}$:\n1. Place an integrator pole at $f = 0$ for zero steady-state DC error.\n2. Place **two zeros** at $f_z = f_c / \\sqrt{K}$ (typically near $0.5 f_0\\text{--}0.8 f_0$) to cancel the $-180^\\circ$ phase drop of the LC double pole.\n3. Place **two high-frequency poles** at $f_p = f_c \\sqrt{K}$ (one cancelling $f_{\\text{ESR}}$, one attenuating $f_{\\text{sw}}/2$ switching ripple).\n\n### 3.3 Digital PID Q-Matching for Light-Load High-$Q$ LC Filters\nAnalog Type-III compensators only synthesize real zeros ($Q \\le 0.5$). At light load, a low-ESR LC filter has $Q_{\\text{plant}} \\gg 0.5$, creating a sharp phase dip. In a digital PID controller, $K_P, K_I, K_D$ are independent and can synthesize **complex conjugate zeros** with $Q_{\\text{PID}} = Q_{\\text{plant}}$:\n$$\\omega_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_I}{K_D}}, \\qquad Q_{\\text{PID}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{K_I K_D}}{K_P}$$\nUse the **3-Tab Interactive Lab** above to plot LLC gain curves, DAB $200\\text{ kW}$ waveforms, and Type-III Bode margins in real time.\n",
  "orcad-pcb": "# 🖥️ High-Speed PCB Layout, Stackup & Signal Integrity: Applied Engineering Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: K. Mitzner, B. Doe, A. Akulin, A. Suponin, and D. Müller, *\"Complete PCB Design Using OrCAD Capture and PCB Editor (2nd Edition),\"* Newnes / Elsevier, 2019.\n\n---\n\n## 1. End-to-End Schematic-to-Fabrication Engineering Flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Sch[\"1. Schematic Capture\\nHierarchical Sheets & CIS BOM\"] --> Stack[\"2. Stackup & Constraints\\nImpedance Z0, Clearances, Rules\"]\n    Stack --> Place[\"3. DFM Placement\\nThermal, Wave/Reflow Orientation\"]\n    Place --> Route[\"4. SI/PI Routing\\nContinuous Return Planes, 3W Rule\"]\n    Route --> Sim[\"5. Pre/Post-Layout SI\\nIBIS Reflections & Eye Diagrams\"]\n    Sim --> CAM[\"6. Artwork & CAM\\nGerber X2 / ODB++, NC Drill, Fab Drawing\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controlled Impedance & Transmission-Line Equations\n\n### 2.1 Critical Trace Length ($l_{\\text{crit}}$)\nA PCB trace behaves as a distributed transmission line (requiring impedance control and termination) whenever its physical length $l$ exceeds half the rising-edge spatial length:\n$$t_{pd} \\approx 85\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}}}\\;\\text{ps/in} \\quad\\left(3.34\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}}}\\;\\text{ps/mm}\\right), \\qquad l_{\\text{crit}} = \\frac{t_r}{2\\,t_{pd}}$$\n- **Rule of Thumb**: For a $1\\text{ ns}$ rise time on FR-4 ($\\varepsilon_{\\text{eff}} \\approx 3.2$, $t_{pd} \\approx 152\\text{ ps/in}$), $l_{\\text{crit}} \\approx 3.3\\text{ in}$ ($84\\text{ mm}$). Fast CMOS/LVDS ($t_r = 0.3\\text{ ns}$) drops $l_{\\text{crit}}$ to $\\sim 1.0\\text{ in}$ ($25\\text{ mm}$).\n\n### 2.2 Surface Microstrip & Symmetric Stripline $Z_0$\n- **Surface Microstrip** (outer layer over reference plane at height $h$, trace width $w$, copper thickness $t$):\n$$Z_{0,\\text{microstrip}} = \\frac{87}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r + 1.41}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5.98\\,h}{0.8\\,w + t}\\right)\\;\\Omega$$\n- **Symmetric Stripline** (inner layer centered between two reference planes separated by $b = 2h + t$):\n$$Z_{0,\\text{stripline}} = \\frac{60}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{4\\,b}{0.67\\,\\pi\\,(0.8\\,w + t)}\\right)\\;\\Omega$$\n\n### 2.3 Reflection Coefficient & Termination Strategy\n$$\\Gamma_L = \\frac{Z_L - Z_0}{Z_L + Z_0}, \\qquad \\Gamma_S = \\frac{Z_S - Z_0}{Z_S + Z_0}$$\n- **Series Source Termination**: Add $R_S = Z_0 - R_{\\text{driver}}$ right at the driver pin (ideal for point-to-point CMOS clocks).\n- **Parallel Load Termination**: Place $R_T = Z_0$ at the receiver (for multi-drop buses or differential LVDS/CML pairs).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Return Current, Crosstalk ($3W$) & Padstack DFM Rules\n\n| Engineering Domain | Design Rule | Failure Prevented |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Return Current Path** | Never route high-speed signals across a split-plane gap; stitch ground vias adjacent to layer-transition signal vias | Dipole EMI radiation & inductive ground bounce ($L\\,di/dt$) |\n| **Crosstalk ($3W$ Rule)** | Center-to-center spacing $S \\ge 3W$ (edge-to-edge $\\ge 2W$) for parallel aggressor/victim traces | Near-end ($K_b$) & far-end crosstalk false triggering |\n| **Decoupling ESL** | Keep power/ground via-pair loop tiny ($< 1.5\\text{ mm}$); use lowest-ESL package (0402/0201) close to IC pins | High-$f_{\\text{SRF}}$ impedance spikes on PDN |\n| **Annular Ring Padstack** | Size pad $D_{\\text{pad}} \\ge D_{\\text{drill}} + 2\\cdot AR_{\\min} + T_{\\text{fab}}$ ($T_{\\text{fab}} \\approx 8\\text{--}12\\text{ mil}$) | Drill breakout & open inner-layer connections |\n| **Thermal Reliefs** | Use 4-spoke thermal reliefs on through-hole ground/power pins (unless high-current $>5\\text{ A}$ requires direct flood) | Cold solder joints during wave/reflow soldering |\n",
  "ipc-610e": "# 🔍 Electronic Assembly Quality & Solder Joint Inspection: Applied IPC-A-610E Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: IPC-A-610E, *\"Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies,\"* Association Connecting Electronics Industries.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Product Class & Four-Tier Disposition Framework\n\n| Product Class | Typical End-Use | Inspection Philosophy |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Class 1** | Consumer electronics, toys, non-critical peripherals | Primary requirement is electrical/mechanical function; cosmetic latitude allowed. |\n| **Class 2** | Industrial controls, servers, telecom, test equipment | Extended life and uninterrupted service desired; moderate dimensional/wetting limits. |\n| **Class 3** | Aerospace, avionics, defense, medical life-support, automotive safety | Continued performance-on-demand is critical; zero tolerance for marginal wetting or breakout. |\n\n**Disposition Hierarchy**: **Target** (Preferred) $\\rightarrow$ **Acceptable** (Pass) $\\rightarrow$ **Process Indicator** (Pass, monitor SPC) $\\rightarrow$ **Defect** (Reject / Rework / MRB).\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMT Dimensional Inspection Criteria (Quick Reference)\n\n### 2.1 Rectangular / Square Chip Components (0201, 0402, 0603, 0805, 1206)\n| Criterion | Symbol | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Max Side Overhang** | $A$ | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | **$\\le 25\\%\\,W$ or $25\\%\\,P$** |\n| **Max End Overhang** | $B$ | **Not permitted ($0$)** | **Not permitted ($0$)** | **Not permitted ($0$)** |\n| **Min End Joint Width** | $C$ | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ or $50\\%\\,P$ | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,W$ or $75\\%\\,P$** |\n| **Min Fillet Height** | $F$ | Wetting evident | $\\ge G + 25\\%\\,H$ or $G + 0.5\\text{ mm}$ | $\\ge G + 25\\%\\,H$ or $G + 0.5\\text{ mm}$ |\n\n### 2.2 Flat Gull-Wing Leads (QFP, SOIC, SOT, TSOP)\n| Criterion | Symbol | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Max Side Overhang** | $A$ | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$ | $\\le 50\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$ | **$\\le 25\\%\\,W$ or $0.5\\text{ mm}$** |\n| **Min End Joint Width** | $C$ | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ | $\\ge 50\\%\\,W$ | **$\\ge 75\\%\\,W$** |\n| **Min Heel Fillet Height** | $F$ | Wetting evident | **$G + 50\\%\\,T$** (mid-lead) | **$G + T$** (top of lead at heel) |\n\n### 2.3 BGA Area Array & Lead-Free (SAC305) Notes\n- **BGA Voiding**: Defect in all classes if X-ray void area in any solder ball exceeds **$25\\%$ of ball projection area** ($\\sim 50\\%$ void diameter).\n- **Lead-Free Appearance**: Dull, matte, or grainy surface texture and slightly steeper wetting angle are **normal and acceptable** for SAC alloys; dimensional fill rules remain identical to SnPb.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Through-Hole Technology (THT) Supported PTH Criteria\n\n| PTH Parameter | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Vertical Solder Fill ($A$)** | Wetting evident | **$\\ge 75\\%$** ($\\ge 50\\%$ on heavy internal ground planes) | **$\\ge 75\\%$** |\n| **Primary Side Barrel Wetting ($B$)** | Wetting evident | **$\\ge 180^\\circ$** | **$\\ge 270^\\circ$** |\n| **Secondary Side Barrel Wetting ($D$)** | $\\ge 270^\\circ$ | $\\ge 270^\\circ$ | **$\\ge 330^\\circ$** |\n| **Secondary Side Land Coverage ($E$)** | $\\ge 75\\%$ | $\\ge 75\\%$ | **$\\ge 75\\%$** |\n| **Lead Protrusion ($L$)** | $\\le 2.5\\text{ mm}$ | $\\le 1.5\\text{ mm}$ | $\\le 1.5\\text{ mm}$ (lead end discernible) |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Applied Shop-Floor Inspection Workflow\n\n1. **Select Magnification by Land Width**: $>1.0\\text{ mm} \\rightarrow 1.75\\times$; $0.5\\text{--}1.0\\text{ mm} \\rightarrow 4\\times$; $0.25\\text{--}0.5\\text{ mm} \\rightarrow 10\\times$; $<0.25\\text{ mm} \\rightarrow 20\\times$.\n2. **Verify EPA Workstation**: Wrist strap ($1\\text{ M}\\Omega$ series resistor), dissipative mat ($10^6\\text{--}10^9\\;\\Omega/\\text{sq}$), no ungrounded plastics within $30\\text{ cm}$.\n3. **Use the Live Joint Inspector Above** to evaluate side overhang, heel fillet height, and PTH fill against Class 1 / 2 / 3 thresholds.\n",
  "ipc-600j": "# 🔬 Bare-Board Fabrication & Microsection Quality: Applied IPC-A-600J Playbook\n\n> **Primary Reference**: IPC-A-600J, *\"Acceptability of Printed Boards,\"* Association Connecting Electronics Industries.\n\n---\n\n## 1. External Bare-Board Acceptance Criteria (Class 1 / 2 / 3)\n\n| Characteristic | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **External Annular Ring (Supported PTH)** | $90^\\circ$ breakout permitted if junction $\\ge 0.05\\text{ mm}$ | $90^\\circ$ breakout permitted in non-critical areas (or $\\ge 0.05\\text{ mm}$) | **$\\ge 0.05\\text{ mm}$ ($2\\text{ mil}$) minimum; $0^\\circ$ breakout** |\n| **PTH Visual Plating Voids** | $\\le 3$ voids/hole, $\\le 10\\%$ of holes | $\\le 1$ void/hole, $\\le 5\\%$ of holes, $<90^\\circ$ arc | **Zero ($0$) plating voids permitted** |\n| **Conductor Width Reduction (Nicks)** | $\\le 30\\%$ of minimum width $W$ | $\\le 20\\%$ of minimum width $W$ | $\\le 20\\%$ of minimum width $W$ |\n| **Measling / Delamination** | Blistering $\\le 1\\%$ area, $\\le 25\\%$ spacing bridge | Blistering $\\le 25\\%$ of conductor spacing | **Delamination $>25\\%$ spacing is Nonconforming** |\n| **Bow & Twist (SMT Boards)** | $\\le 0.75\\%$ | $\\le 0.75\\%$ | $\\le 0.75\\%$ |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Internal Microsection Acceptance Criteria ($288^\\circ\\text{C} / 10\\text{ s}$ Thermal Stress)\n\nCross-sectional metallography verifies internal copper-to-barrel interconnect integrity after thermal shock:\n\n| Microsection Feature | Class 1 | Class 2 | Class 3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Internal Annular Ring** | $90^\\circ$ breakout acceptable | Tangency ($0\\text{ mm}$) or $90^\\circ$ per spec | **$\\ge 0.025\\text{ mm}$ ($1.0\\text{ mil}$) minimum; $0^\\circ$ breakout** |\n| **PTH Copper Plating Thickness** | $\\ge 20\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ avg ($18\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ min) | $\\ge 20\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ avg ($18\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ min) | **$\\ge 25\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ ($1.0\\text{ mil}$) avg, $20\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ min** |\n| **Copper Wicking** | $\\le 125\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ ($5.0\\text{ mil}$) | $\\le 100\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ ($4.0\\text{ mil}$) | **$\\le 80\\;\\mu\\text{m}$ ($3.15\\text{ mil}$)** (never violates min dielectric spacing) |\n| **Positive Etchback** | Optional | $0.005\\text{--}0.080\\text{ mm}$ if specified | $0.005\\text{--}0.080\\text{ mm}$ ($0.2\\text{--}3.15\\text{ mil}$) without shadowing |\n| **Foil / Barrel Cracks (A, B, C, E, F)** | Surface foil \"A\" crack permitted if non-propagating | **Barrel \"E\" & Corner \"F\" cracks Nonconforming** | **All Barrel \"E\", Corner \"F\" & Inner-Layer \"B\" cracks Nonconforming** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Failure-Mode Root-Cause Matrix for Bare-Board QA\n\n| Microsection Defect | Probable Fabrication Root Cause | Corrective Engineering Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inner-Layer Separation (\"B\" / Smear)** | Incomplete desmear or excessive drill heat smearing epoxy over inner copper | Tune plasma/permanganate desmear cycle; reduce drill chipload |\n| **Corner \"F\" / Barrel \"E\" Cracks** | Low copper elongation ($<12\\text{--}18\\%$) or high Z-axis CTE mismatch during $288^\\circ\\text{C}$ solder shock | Increase acid-copper bath organic brightener control; use high-$T_g$ ($\\ge 170^\\circ\\text{C}$) laminate |\n| **Excessive Annular Ring Breakout** | Drill wander, inner-layer artwork scaling mismatch, or undersized padstack | Increase padstack diameter ($D_{\\text{pad}} = D_{\\text{drill}} + 2\\cdot AR_{\\min} + T_{\\text{fab}}$) or add teardrops |\n| **Excessive Copper Wicking** | Aggressive glass-weave fracturing during drilling + excessive etchback | Reduce drill feed rate / replace worn drill bits; tighten etchback dwell |\n"
};
